Here's the final program:

```csharp
using System.Text.Json;
using System.Text.Json.Serialization;
using System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata;

var options = new JsonSerializerOptions
{
    TypeInfoResolver = new DefaultJsonTypeInfoResolver()
};

// 1) Strongly-typed: GetTypeInfo<T>() → JsonTypeInfo<T>
JsonTypeInfo<Person> personInfo = options.GetTypeInfo<Person>();
var json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new Person("Alice", 30), personInfo);
Person p = JsonSerializer.Deserialize(json, personInfo)!;

// 2) Probe without throwing: TryGetTypeInfo(Type, out JsonTypeInfo?)
bool found = options.TryGetTypeInfo(typeof(Person), out JsonTypeInfo? info);
// Returns false (not an exception) when the resolver can't provide metadata.
```

**Key APIs:**

| Goal | Method | Returns |
|------|--------|---------|
| Typed metadata | `options.GetTypeInfo<T>()` | `JsonTypeInfo<T>` |
| Check-without-throw | `options.TryGetTypeInfo(typeof(T), out var info)` | `bool` (`false` if unavailable) |

Both were introduced in .NET 9 and are available on `net11.0`. `TryGetTypeInfo` returns `false` when the configured resolver yields `null` for that type — no exception, no try/catch needed.
